  "title": "Fallbacks became a cross-modal feature",
  "summary": "The API got better at combining media tools, describing what models accept, and recovering when a provider does not cooperate.\n\nCommunity model owners can configure ordered backups for retryable upstream failures. The routing work grew from text and image to video, model-selecting audio, embeddings, and 3D; no registry fallback pairs were added by default. FLUX.1 Schnell also gained a Replicate fallback, while image-input failures now return stable HTTP 400 errors. Failed attempts are not charged, and a successful backup costs no more than the requested model.\n\nThe multimodal agent introduced as polli-agent became Weaver. Its OpenAI-compatible chat endpoint can stream responses and coordinate text, image, video, audio, search, and media tools. Enabled community agents can also generate within a caller’s model access and budget without receiving the caller’s API key. Clients using the earlier `polli` model ID need to switch to `weaver`.\n\nElevenLabs additions cover multi-speaker dialogue, voice changing, speech isolation, and speech with character-level timestamps. Kokoro 82M added 54 voices across eight languages, AssemblyAI transcription moved to Universal-3.5 Pro while retaining older IDs as aliases, and `gpt-realtime-2.1-mini` joined the realtime route.\n\nCommunity image models that support reference-image editing can advertise `/v1/images/edits`; generation-only models keep their existing behavior. Owners can now declare accepted input modalities in the model dialog, API, or CLI, so the catalog gives callers a better idea of what to send before they send it.",
  "pr_count": 70,
